---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const timeline = [
  {
    date: "1775",
    title: "Named to Feed an Army",
    description: "Colonel Henry Champion is selected as a Commissary for the Revolutionary War effort.",
    href: "#colonel",
  },
  {
    date: "1777\u201378",
    title: "300 Miles to Valley Forge",
    description: "Colonel Champion drives a cattle herd to Washington's starving army in the brutal winter.",
    href: "#colonel",
  },
  {
    date: "1779",
    title: "The Capture of Stony Point",
    description: "General Henry Champion serves under Washington's direct command in the celebrated night assault.",
    href: "#general",
  },
  {
    date: "1790",
    title: "The House Is Built",
    description: "Colonel Champion builds the house in Colchester's Westchester section for his son, General Henry Champion.",
    href: "#built",
  },
  {
    date: "1797",
    title: "Passed to the Next Champion",
    description: "Colonel Champion dies; formal ownership of the house passes to his son, General Henry Champion.",
    href: "#built",
  },
  {
    date: "1836",
    title: "End of an Era",
    description: "General Henry Champion dies at the house, closing 46 years of Champion family residence.",
    href: "#general",
  },
  {
    date: "1972",
    title: "Nationally Recognized",
    description: "The house is added to the National and State Registers of Historic Places.",
    href: "#register",
  },
  {
    date: "2024",
    title: "A House Nearly Lost",
    description: "Facing foreclosure after years of vacancy, the General Henry Champion Trust steps in to save it.",
    href: "/restoration#rescue",
  },
  {
    date: "2026",
    title: "Restoration Begins",
    description: "New roofs go on the house and barn \u2014 the first, most urgent phase of bringing it back.",
    href: "/restoration#today",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="History"
  description="The story of the General Henry Champion House, Colonel Henry Champion, General Henry Champion, and the Champion family of Colchester, Connecticut."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="relative">
    <img
      src="/images/history-archival.webp"
      alt="Archival photograph of the General Henry Champion House"
      class="h-[50vh] w-full object-cover"
      width="969"
      height="671"
      fetchpriority="high"
      loading="eager"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-black/85 via-black/45 to-black/5"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 flex items-end">
      <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-6 pb-12 text-white [text-shadow:0_2px_10px_rgba(0,0,0,0.55)]">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl">A Colonel's House, a General's Home</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-xl text-lg text-white/90">
          Two Champions, a Revolutionary War, and 230 years of history on Westchester Road.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Timeline -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16">
    <p class="eyebrow text-[var(--color-brick)]">At a Glance</p>
    <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl">230 Years, in Brief</h2>
    <ol class="relative mt-8 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-stone)] pl-8">
      {timeline.map((event) => (
        <li class="relative pb-10 last:pb-0">
          <span class="absolute -left-[2.35rem] top-1 h-4 w-4 rounded-full border-2 border-[var(--color-brick)] bg-[var(--color-cream)]"></span>
          <a href={event.href} class="group block">
            <span class="eyebrow text-[var(--color-brick)]">{event.date}</span>
            <h3 class="mt-1 text-lg group-hover:text-[var(--color-brick)]">{event.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">{event.description}</p>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16">
    <section id="built">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Built by a Colonel, for His Son</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        The house was built in 1790 by Colonel Henry Champion, a Revolutionary War officer, as a home for
        his son, General Henry Champion. It was designed by William Spratt, the same house joiner who
        designed the Epaphroditus Champion House in East Haddam and the Deming House in Litchfield.
        General Henry Champion made the house his home for the rest of his life, until his own death in
        1836 &mdash; the 46-year tenure that gives the house the name it carries today. Colonel Champion,
        who commissioned the house, died in 1797.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="colonel" class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Colonel Henry Champion &mdash; Feeding an Army</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        Colonel Henry Champion (1723&ndash;1797) was born in East Haddam, the grandson of an original
        settler of Saybrook. He entered the colonial militia in 1741, reached the rank of Lieutenant by
        1750, and served in the French and Indian War before the Revolution began. In 1775, the Connecticut
        General Assembly selected him as a Commissary for the war effort, tasked with procuring goods and
        supplying the Continental Army &mdash; work that put him in close, regular contact with General
        Washington and Connecticut Governor Jonathan Trumbull. By 1778, he was appointed sole Commissary
        General for the Eastern Department.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        His best-known act came in the brutal winter of 1777&ndash;78: working with his son Epaphroditus,
        he personally drove a herd of cattle 300 miles to reach the starving Continental Army at Valley
        Forge, purchased at great expense and eaten by the troops within five days of arrival.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        Family tradition holds that his daughter, Deborah Champion, once carried dispatches on horseback
        from New London to General Washington in Boston &mdash; passing safely through British lines, the
        story goes, because she was a woman.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="general" class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">General Henry Champion &mdash; A Life at the House</h2>
      <figure class="float-none mb-6 mt-4 md:float-right md:mb-4 md:ml-8 md:w-72">
        <img
          src="/images/gallery/portrait-general-henry-champion-thumb.webp"
          data-full-src="/images/gallery/portrait-general-henry-champion.webp"
          alt="Portrait of General Henry Champion, seated in a dark coat with a white cravat, pointing to a paper on a table"
          class="w-full cursor-zoom-in rounded-lg object-cover shadow-md"
          data-lightbox
          width="1070"
          height="1410"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-[var(--color-charcoal)]/80">
          Portrait of General Henry Champion
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        General Henry Champion (1751&ndash;1836) was born and died in this part of Colchester, then known
        as Westchester. He answered the Lexington Alarm in 1775 and fought at Bunker Hill. In 1779, as
        acting major of the First Battalion, Light Brigade, he served under Washington's direct command at
        the capture of Stony Point &mdash; a moment he marked every year afterward, hosting a "Stony Point
        Day" celebration at this house each July 15 for the rest of his life.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        After the war, he remained active in public life: a deputy to the Connecticut General Assembly, an
        Assistant to the Governor, a judge on the state's Supreme Court of Errors, and a deacon of the
        Westchester church. He was also a major investor in the Connecticut Land Company &mdash; the towns
        of Champion, New York and Champion, Ohio are named for him, and his portrait at the Wadsworth
        Atheneum shows him with a map of the Western Reserve lands he helped settle.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        He is buried in Westchester Cemetery on Cemetery Road in Colchester, his grave marked by a
        classical white marble obelisk.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture" class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">An Architectural Record</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        The house's Federal-period details are well documented in its 1972 National Register nomination: a
        gambrel roof with a long rear slope nearly reaching a lean-to, twin brick chimneys, an
        Ionic-columned and triangular-pedimented entry portico, matching pedimented dormers, and
        twelve-over-twelve sash windows throughout the main house. A one-and-a-half-story ell extends to
        the south, later fitted with a conservatory. A historic stone wall with iron gates still marks the
        property's edge along the road.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        Spratt's own story is nearly as improbable as the house he built. A Scottish-born soldier in the
        British Royal Artillery, he was captured at the 1777 Battle of Saratoga and spent three years as a
        prisoner of war in Hartford and Farmington before choosing to stay in Connecticut rather than
        return home. He went on to design some of the region's finest Federal and Georgian houses,
        including &ldquo;The Terraces,&rdquo; General Epaphroditus Champion's riverside estate in East
        Haddam, still admired for its sweeping stone terraces above the Connecticut River.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <span class="eyebrow rounded-full border border-[var(--color-brick)] px-4 py-2 text-[var(--color-brick)]">
          Federal-Period Design, 1790
        </span>
        <span class="eyebrow rounded-full border border-[var(--color-brick)] px-4 py-2 text-[var(--color-brick)]">
          Master Builder: William Spratt
        </span>
        <span class="eyebrow rounded-full border border-[var(--color-brick)] px-4 py-2 text-[var(--color-brick)]">
          National Register of Historic Places, 1972
        </span>
        <span class="eyebrow rounded-full border border-[var(--color-brick)] px-4 py-2 text-[var(--color-brick)]">
          Washington&ndash;Rochambeau Revolutionary Route
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <img
          src="/images/gallery/portico-with-columns-thumb.webp"
          data-full-src="/images/gallery/portico-with-columns.webp"
          alt="The Ionic-columned, triangular-pedimented entry portico"
          class="h-80 w-full cursor-zoom-in rounded-lg object-cover shadow-sm"
          data-lightbox
          width="647"
          height="900"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/gallery/ionic-capital-detail-thumb.webp"
          data-full-src="/images/gallery/ionic-capital-detail.webp"
          alt="Close-up detail of an Ionic column capital on the entry portico"
          class="h-80 w-full cursor-zoom-in rounded-lg object-cover shadow-sm"
          data-lightbox
          width="900"
          height="712"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="register" class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Nationally Recognized</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        The Champion House holds two federal historic designations. It was added to the National and State
        Registers of Historic Places on October 10, 1972 (NR reference #72001323). It's also a stop on the
        Washington&ndash;Rochambeau Revolutionary Route, a National Park Service&ndash;designated National
        Historic Trail retracing the march of the Continental and French armies in 1781 and 1782.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-stone)] bg-[var(--color-parchment)] px-8 py-8">
      <p class="eyebrow text-[var(--color-brick)]">For Educators</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl">Bring This House Into the Classroom</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
        Teaching local history or the American Revolution? Download a free one-page classroom resource on
        the Champion family, the house's Federal-period architecture, and its connection to the
        Washington&ndash;Rochambeau Revolutionary Route &mdash; including a timeline and discussion questions
        for students.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/downloads/general-henry-champion-house-classroom-resource.pdf"
        download
        class="btn-primary mt-6 inline-block"
      >Download the Classroom Resource (PDF)</a>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 border-t border-[var(--color-stone)] pt-8 text-sm text-[var(--color-charcoal)]">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-2 text-[var(--color-brick)]">Sources / Further Reading</p>
      <p>
        Much of this history comes from the Colchester Historical Society's own research on the Champion
        family, along with the original 1972 National Register nomination. For more, visit the <a
          href="https://www.colchesterhistory.org/museum/exhibits/the-story-of-the-champion-family/"
          class="text-[var(--color-brick)] underline"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Colchester Historical Society</a>. Additional archival photographs of the house, taken for the
        Library of Congress's Historic American Buildings Survey (HABS CT-143), are available through the <a
          href="https://www.loc.gov/resource/hhh.ct0216.photos"
          class="text-[var(--color-brick)] underline"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Library of Congress</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
